{"reference":"John 3","book":"John","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Interpretive Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Several features of John 3 resist overly tidy reading. The word translated “anew” in Jesus’ conversation with Nicodemus can also mean “from above”: Nicodemus hears it as a second birth, while the discourse goes on to speak of the Spirit and of the one who comes from heaven. Greek manuscripts also do not mark a clear point at which Jesus’ speech ends and the narrator’s exposition begins in 3:16-21, so readers differ over the speaker of those verses. The chapter’s final scene continues John the Baptist’s testimony that he is not the Messiah and points beyond himself to Jesus; elsewhere in this witness, the title “Lamb of God” may draw on more than one set of associations rather than one certain image.","verse_refs":["John 3:1-15","John 3:16-21","John 3:22-36"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_003_f48e6d52c1be055d4b89c04c"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Jesus’ reference to Moses lifting up the serpent in the wilderness is a later biblical reception of that wilderness story. It should therefore be distinguished from interpreting the story in Numbers within its own Torah setting; John 3 uses the image for its present argument about the Son of Man and life.","verse_refs":["John 3:14-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_003_8b12502273d746563b440082"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}